# 10. Describe the Pod

This is one of the **most important commands for troubleshooting**.

```bash
kubectl describe pod nginx-pod
```

Students will see:

```text
Name:
Namespace:
Node:
Status:
IP:
Containers:
  nginx-container
    Image:
    State:
    Ready:
Events:
```

Tell students:

> Whenever a Pod isn't working, `kubectl describe pod` is one of the first commands you should use.

---

# 11. View Pod Logs

Run:

```bash
kubectl logs nginx-pod
```

For Nginx, you may see logs such as HTTP requests after accessing it.

Explain:

```text
Application
    ↓
Container
    ↓
Logs
    ↓
kubectl logs
```

If there are multiple containers:

```bash
kubectl logs <pod-name> -c <container-name>
```

---

# 12. Execute Commands Inside the Pod

This is another important DevOps skill.

Run:

```bash
kubectl exec -it nginx-pod -- /bin/bash
```

If Bash isn't available:

```bash
kubectl exec -it nginx-pod -- /bin/sh
```

Now you're inside the container.

Try:

```bash
hostname
```

Then:

```bash
ls
```

Then:

```bash
cat /etc/os-release
```

Exit:

```bash
exit
```

Explain the difference:

```text
kubectl exec
     ↓
Execute command
     ↓
Inside container
```

This is extremely useful for troubleshooting.

---

# 13. Add a Port to the Pod

Now update the YAML:

```yaml
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod

metadata:
  name: nginx-pod

spec:
  containers:
    - name: nginx-container
      image: nginx:latest
      ports:
        - containerPort: 80
```

Apply:

```bash
kubectl apply -f pod.yaml
```

Then:

```bash
kubectl describe pod nginx-pod
```

You should see:

```text
Port: 80/TCP
```

### Important clarification for students

This:

```yaml
containerPort: 80
```

**does not expose the Pod to the Internet.**

It documents/declares the container port.

Actual application exposure will be taught using **Service**.

---

# 14. Important Pod Commands

Give students this table:

| Task              | Command                                 |
| ----------------- | --------------------------------------- |
| List Pods         | `kubectl get pods`                      |
| Detailed Pod list | `kubectl get pods -o wide`              |
| Create/Update     | `kubectl apply -f pod.yaml`             |
| Describe          | `kubectl describe pod nginx-pod`        |
| Logs              | `kubectl logs nginx-pod`                |
| Execute command   | `kubectl exec -it nginx-pod -- /bin/sh` |
| Delete            | `kubectl delete pod nginx-pod`          |
| YAML output       | `kubectl get pod nginx-pod -o yaml`     |
| Watch status      | `kubectl get pods -w`                   |

---

# 15. Delete the Pod

Run:

```bash
kubectl delete pod nginx-pod
```

Expected:

```text
pod "nginx-pod" deleted
```

Then:

```bash
kubectl get pods
```

You should get:

```text
No resources found in default namespace.
```

This is a good teaching moment.

Ask:

> "If the Pod is deleted, will Kubernetes recreate it?"

Answer:

**No.**

Why?

Because we created a **standalone Pod**.

There is no Deployment or ReplicaSet managing it.

This naturally creates curiosity for **Day 3 — ReplicaSet and Deployment**.

---

# 16. Pod Lifecycle

Introduce these states:

```text
Pending
   ↓
Running
   ↓
Succeeded / Failed
```

Students should also recognize:

```text
ContainerCreating
CrashLoopBackOff
ImagePullBackOff
ErrImagePull
Completed
```

Don't go deeply into troubleshooting all of these today.

Just tell them:

> These statuses are important when diagnosing Pods.

---

# 17. Common Beginner Error — YAML

Since you're using Killercoda, students will probably make YAML mistakes.

For example:

```yaml
metadata:
name: nginx-pod
```

❌ Incorrect indentation.

Correct:

```yaml
metadata:
  name: nginx-pod
```

Another common mistake:

```yaml
spec:
containers:
```

Correct:

```yaml
spec:
  containers:
```

Tell them:

> **YAML is indentation-sensitive.**

Before applying:

```bash
kubectl apply -f pod.yaml
```

they should carefully check indentation.

